examples.solution_text:
Split each quarter into $2$ equal parts by dividing the bar horizontally.

[tikz]% mathsmap-diagram-colours {"version":1,"kind":"geometry","base":[],"semantic":[{"name":"templateShade","hex":"D4E8FF","reason":"Solution-only horizontal partition splits each original quarter into two equal parts. Preserve the question whole dimensions and left three-quarters shaded region."}],"reference":"template split-solution-eighths"}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1mm,y=1mm]
\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes="1"/>}
\definecolor{templateShade}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\path[use as bounding box] (-0.6,-16.6) rectangle (60.6,0.6);
\fill[templateShade] (0,-8) rectangle (15,0);
\fill[templateShade] (15,-8) rectangle (30,0);
\fill[templateShade] (30,-8) rectangle (45,0);
\fill[templateShade] (0,-16) rectangle (15,-8);
\fill[templateShade] (15,-16) rectangle (30,-8);
\fill[templateShade] (30,-16) rectangle (45,-8);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (15,0)--(15,-16);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (30,0)--(30,-16);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (45,0)--(45,-16);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (0,-8)--(60,-8);
\draw[black,line width=0.8pt] (0,0) rectangle (60,-16);
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

There are now $8$ equal parts, with $6$ shaded. The shaded amount stays the same.
$\frac{3}{4}=\frac{3\times2}{4\times2}=\frac{6}{8}$
